<span id="A4.T6.3.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Clayton</span>
<span id="A4.T6.3.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.T6.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.676" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.676</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.676</annotation></semantics></math></span>
<span id="A4.T6.3.2.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.T6.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.647" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.647</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.647</annotation></semantics></math></span>
<span id="A4.T6.3.2.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.T6.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.647" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.647</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.647</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>
<span id="A4.T6.3.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="A4.T6.3.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb">Gumbel</span>
<span id="A4.T6.3.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><math id="A4.T6.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.632" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.632</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.632</annotation></semantics></math></span>
<span id="A4.T6.3.2.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><math id="A4.T6.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.618" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.618</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.618</annotation></semantics></math></span>
<span id="A4.T6.3.2.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><math id="A4.T6.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.618" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.618</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.618</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>
</span></p></div></div>
</figure>
<div id="A4.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.p8.1" class="ltx_p">The results in Table <a href="#A4.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ Appendix D Validating the Gaussian Copula Assumption ‣ Can LLMs Refuse Questions They Do Not Know? Measuring Knowledge-Aware Refusal in Factual Tasks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> show that the Gaussian copula provides the strongest average fit. After accounting for complexity, it provides the best overall trade-off between parsimony and data fit. The Student-<em id="A4.p8.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">t</em> copula, despite its additional heavy-tail parameter, does not improve the average log-likelihood and is uniformly worse once complexity penalties are applied. This aligns with intuition for 2 <math id="A4.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> 2 data with fixed margins, where heavy tails are weakly identified and tend to degenerate toward the Gaussian case. The asymmetric Clayton and Gumbel copulas trail substantially on both raw fit and information criteria, though they can win occasionally on individual units.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conclusion.</span> We choose the Gaussian copula for two primary reasons: (1) it provides the better average fit across model-prompt combinations as evidenced by superior log-likelihood, AIC, and BIC scores; and (2) it is the simplest and most interpretable copula family, requiring only a single correlation parameter while making minimal distributional assumptions about the dependence structure.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Consequently, the bivariate normal copula is both simple and sufficiently accurate for the refusal–incorrectness dependence considered here. Its combination of low assumptions and competitive fit makes it a natural default for estimating the Refusal Index.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix E </span>Fixed Endpoints and Shape of Iso-RI Curves</h2>

<div id="A5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We derive two key properties of the accuracy–refusal curve used in the paper:
(i) every iso-RI curve passes through the same two endpoints at refusal <math id="A5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=1</annotation></semantics></math>; and
(ii) when the association between <em id="A5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">wrongness</em> and the <em id="A5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">refusal score</em> is stronger (i.e., larger RI), the curve is higher in its interior, creating more curvature relative to the straight line joining its endpoints.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="A5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Z_{R},Z_{W})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Z_{R},Z_{W})</annotation></semantics></math> be jointly standard normal with correlation <math id="A5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho\in(-1,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho\in(-1,1)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Fix thresholds <math id="A5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{r},\tau_{w}\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{r},\tau_{w}\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> and define</p>
<table id="A5.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="R:=\mathbf{1}\{Z_{R}&gt;\tau_{r}\}\quad\text{(refuse)},\qquad W:=\mathbf{1}\{Z_{W}&gt;\tau_{w}\}\quad\text{(wrong under forced answering)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>:=</mo><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mtext>(refuse)</mtext><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>:=</mo><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mtext>(wrong under forced answering)</mtext><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R:=\mathbf{1}\{Z_{R}&gt;\tau_{r}\}\quad\text{(refuse)},\qquad W:=\mathbf{1}\{Z_{W}&gt;\tau_{w}\}\quad\text{(wrong under forced answering)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The refusal rate is <math id="A5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r:=\Pr(R=1)=1-\Phi(\tau_{r})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>Pr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r:=\Pr(R=1)=1-\Phi(\tau_{r})</annotation></semantics></math>.
The unconditional error rate is <math id="A5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:=\Pr(W=1)=1-\Phi(\tau_{w})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>Pr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:=\Pr(W=1)=1-\Phi(\tau_{w})</annotation></semantics></math>, so the correct answer rate (at <math id="A5.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=0</annotation></semantics></math>) is <math id="A5.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu:=1-\pi=\Phi(\tau_{w})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu:=1-\pi=\Phi(\tau_{w})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A5.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> is the standard normal CDF.
For a given <math id="A5.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> we take <math id="A5.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{r}=\Phi^{-1}(1-r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{r}=\Phi^{-1}(1-r)</annotation></semantics></math>.
We define the <em id="A5.p2.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">correct answer rate</em> at refusal <math id="A5.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> as</p>
<table id="A5.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a(r;\rho)\ :=\ \Pr(\text{correct and answered})\ =\ \Pr(W=0,\ R=0)\ =\ \Phi_{2}\!\bigl(\tau_{r},\ \tau_{w};\ \rho\bigr)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.778em">:=</mo><mrow><mi>Pr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>correct and answered</mtext><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.778em">=</mo><mrow><mi>Pr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.778em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a(r;\rho)\ :=\ \Pr(\text{correct and answered})\ =\ \Pr(W=0,\ R=0)\ =\ \Phi_{2}\!\bigl(\tau_{r},\ \tau_{w};\ \rho\bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A5.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{2}(\cdot,\cdot;\rho)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{2}(\cdot,\cdot;\rho)</annotation></semantics></math> is the bivariate standard normal CDF with correlation <math id="A5.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>.
We orient the score so that higher <math id="A5.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{R}</annotation></semantics></math> means “more refuse” for items more likely to be wrong (the intended setting for RI, typically <math id="A5.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 1 (Endpoints).</span>
For any <math id="A5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{w}</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="A5.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a(0;\rho)=\mu\qquad\text{and}\qquad a(1;\rho)=0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a(0;\rho)=\mu\qquad\text{and}\qquad a(1;\rho)=0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A5.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="A5.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Proof.</em>
At <math id="A5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=0</annotation></semantics></math> we have <math id="A5.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{r}=+\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{r}=+\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, hence
<math id="A5.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a(0;\rho)=\Phi_{2}(+\infty,\tau_{w};\rho)=\Phi(\tau_{w})=\mu." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a(0;\rho)=\Phi_{2}(+\infty,\tau_{w};\rho)=\Phi(\tau_{w})=\mu.</annotation></semantics></math>
At <math id="A5.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=1</annotation></semantics></math> we have <math id="A5.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{r}=-\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{r}=-\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, hence
<math id="A5.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a(1;\rho)=\Phi_{2}(-\infty,\tau_{w};\rho)=0." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a(1;\rho)=\Phi_{2}(-\infty,\tau_{w};\rho)=0.</annotation></semantics></math>
∎</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="A5.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Monotonicity in <math id="A5.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>.</em>
Since <math id="A5.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{r}=\Phi^{-1}(1-r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{r}=\Phi^{-1}(1-r)</annotation></semantics></math> is strictly decreasing in <math id="A5.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A5.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is increasing in each argument, <math id="A5.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a(r;\rho)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a(r;\rho)</annotation></semantics></math> is strictly decreasing in <math id="A5.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> for fixed <math id="A5.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.p6.1" class="ltx_p">This makes intuitive sense:
at <math id="A5.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=0</annotation></semantics></math> we answer everything, so correct answer rate equals the model’s overall accuracy <math id="A5.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>.
As <math id="A5.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\to 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\to 1</annotation></semantics></math> we answer almost nothing, so the correct answer rate approaches <math id="A5.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 2 (Monotonicity in <math id="A5.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>).</span>
Fix any interior refusal level <math id="A5.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then <math id="A5.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a(r;\rho)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a(r;\rho)</annotation></semantics></math> in equation <a href="#A5.E8" title="Equation 8 ‣ Appendix E Fixed Endpoints and Shape of Iso-RI Curves ‣ Can LLMs Refuse Questions They Do Not Know? Measuring Knowledge-Aware Refusal in Factual Tasks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> is strictly increasing in <math id="A5.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="A5.p8.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Proof.</em>
With <math id="A5.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> fixed, <math id="A5.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> is fixed, and
<math id="A5.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a(r;\rho)=\Phi_{2}(\tau_{r},\tau_{w};\rho)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a(r;\rho)=\Phi_{2}(\tau_{r},\tau_{w};\rho).</annotation></semantics></math>
The standard identity
<math id="A5.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\partial}{\partial\rho}\Phi_{2}(x,y;\rho)=\varphi_{2}(x,y;\rho)&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>φ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial}{\partial\rho}\Phi_{2}(x,y;\rho)=\varphi_{2}(x,y;\rho)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>
implies
<math id="A5.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{d}{d\rho}a(r;\rho)=\varphi_{2}(\tau_{r},\tau_{w};\rho)&gt;0." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>φ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{d}{d\rho}a(r;\rho)=\varphi_{2}(\tau_{r},\tau_{w};\rho)&gt;0.</annotation></semantics></math>
∎</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary (Higher curves with higher RI).</span>
All accuracy–refusal curves share endpoints <math id="A5.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r,a)=(0,\mu)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r,a)=(0,\mu)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A5.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,0)</annotation></semantics></math> by Proposition 1.
If <math id="A5.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{2}&gt;\rho_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{2}&gt;\rho_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e., higher RI), then by Proposition 2,
<math id="A5.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a(r;\rho_{2})&gt;a(r;\rho_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a(r;\rho_{2})&gt;a(r;\rho_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>
for every <math id="A5.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus the higher-RI curve lies strictly above the lower-RI curve throughout the interior while meeting it at the endpoints, creating greater upward curvature relative to the straight line between <math id="A5.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(0,\mu)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(0,\mu)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A5.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,0)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.p10.1" class="ltx_p">The intuition is straightforward: at a fixed refusal level, the key factor in equation <a href="#A5.E8" title="Equation 8 ‣ Appendix E Fixed Endpoints and Shape of Iso-RI Curves ‣ Can LLMs Refuse Questions They Do Not Know? Measuring Knowledge-Aware Refusal in Factual Tasks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> is the joint tail probability <math id="A5.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{11}(\rho)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>11</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{11}(\rho)</annotation></semantics></math>.
As <math id="A5.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> increases, wrong items and high-refusal items occur together more often, making the kept (non-refused) set cleaner. This increases the correct answer rate at every interior <math id="A5.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since the endpoints are fixed, the entire curve shifts upward.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A6" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix F </span>Results on SimpleQA</h2>

<div id="A6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We provide metrics on all models on SimpleQA in Table <a href="#A6.T7" title="Table 7 ‣ Appendix F Results on SimpleQA ‣ Can LLMs Refuse Questions They Do Not Know? Measuring Knowledge-Aware Refusal in Factual Tasks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>, the 95% CI is computed by bootstrap with 1000 samples.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A6.T7" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 7: </span>Results on SimpleQA with 95% CI.</figcaption>
<table id="A6.T7.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="A6.T7.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A6.T7.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt">
<span id="A6.T7.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top">
<span id="A6.T7.2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.T7.2.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Model</span></span>
</span></td>
<td id="A6.T7.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt"><span id="A6.T7.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Correct Answer Rate</span></td>
<td id="A6.T7.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt"><span id="A6.T7.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Refusal</span></td>
<td id="A6.T7.2.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt"><span id="A6.T7.2.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">C / A</span></td>
<td id="A6.T7.2.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt"><span id="A6.T7.2.1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">F-score</span></td>
<td id="A6.T7.2.1.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt"><span id="A6.T7.2.1.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Weighted</span></td>
<td id="A6.T7.2.1.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt"><span id="A6.T7.2.1.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Refusal Index</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A6.T7.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A6.T7.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">
<span id="A6.T7.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top">
